- Solid coordinate axes; upward positive-y and rightward positive-x directions.
- Readable labels: y, 1 above the horizontal axis, origin 0/O, -1 below it, and x at the right edge.
- No additional numeric x labels or other numeric y labels are confidently readable.
- Some horizontal grid strokes near label cutouts appear broken; do not classify those as dashed mathematical boundaries.
- Source subpanel label `б)` occupies the lower-left unhatched corner.

### Boundary, region, and endpoints

- One steeply rising straight boundary, from the bottom frame left of the vertical axis to the top frame right of it.
- The visible spine appears solid/continuous. Dense descending hatching abuts its right side; no intentional repeating dash pattern is resolved.
- The boundary meets the horizontal axis at the first grid track to its right (half a unit).
- It meets the vertical axis near the labeled -1 level (two half-unit rows below the horizontal axis). The junction is crowded by the axis and adjacent hatching, so its exact center is less secure than the x-axis crossing.
- The top clip appears near x = 2 and the bottom clip near x = -1 on the half-unit lattice. These are provisional unlabeled clip readings, not additional source point labels.
- The hatched side is right/southeast of the steep line; it includes the positive-x side of the horizontal axis beyond the crossing. The left/northwest side is unhatched.
- Hatching is made of closely spaced descending diagonal strokes, not a second family of inequality boundaries.
- Top/bottom terminations are frame clips. No endpoint circles/dots, line arrowheads, or separate right vertical mathematical boundary are visible.

### Unresolved items

- The y-axis crossing and top/bottom frame junctions are visually crowded; their exact pixel placement is not certified.
- The native crop supports a continuous visible boundary, but does not expose every boundary pixel under adjoining hatch. Strictness is not adjudicated in this checkpoint.
- The minus stroke in -1 is short and visually close to surrounding ruled strokes; retain the crowded-label warning.

## Fig. 27a: left panel

### Grid, axes, and labels

- One rectangular coordinate panel, approximately x pixels 33 to 846 and y pixels 38 to 600; origin approximately (440, 318).
- Grid extent: 18 columns, 12 rows; 9 columns on either side of the vertical axis, 6 rows above/below the horizontal axis, half-unit steps, zero phase.
- The lattice continues through the circle's unhatched central band and outside the circle. Some hatch-covered grid portions remain visible, rather than an entirely blank shaded field.
- Solid x/y axes with grid-intersection ticks and positive-direction axis labeling.
- Readable labels: y, x, origin 0/O, y = 1 above the origin, x = 1 to the right of the origin, and x = 2 near/right of the circle's rightmost intersection.
- No negative numeric label or y = 2 / -2 label is confidently readable. Circle/grid positions are not a license to invent those printed labels.
- Source `а)` is at the lower-left corner; no named points or radius label are visible.

### Boundaries, region, and endpoints

- One apparently circular outline centered at the axis intersection. Its horizontal and vertical radii span four half-unit cells (two coordinate units).
- Observed cardinal positions: left (-2, 0), right (2, 0), top (0, 2), bottom (0, -2). These are unlabeled positions determined by the readable scale and grid, not a supplied equation.
- Two vertical internal boundaries, two half-unit columns left and right of the y axis, respectively: x = -1 and x = 1.
- Each vertical boundary stops at its upper and lower intersections with the circle. The intersection heights lie between the y = 1.5 and y = 2 rows above and between y = -2 and y = -1.5 below. No exact radical or finer numerical height is printed; none is inferred here.
- The left circular cap between the left arc and the left vertical boundary is hatched. The matching right cap between the right vertical boundary and the right arc is also hatched.
- The central vertical band inside the circle is unhatched, as is the exterior of the circle. The two hatched components are disconnected; this is not the whole disk, the central strip, or a region outside the circle.
- Hatching rises diagonally to the right, unlike the descending hatching in fig. 26.
- The vertical cap boundaries and most of the circular perimeter appear solid. The upper-right circular arc has locally thin/gappy reproduction; this single observation cannot determine whether every gap is a print defect or an intended stroke distinction.
- No open endpoint circles, filled endpoint dots, arrowheads, or separately named intersection points are visible. Circle/vertical intersections are geometric junctions only.
- Minor scan distortion does not independently establish an ellipse or unequal mathematical radii.

### Unresolved items

- The stroke continuity of the upper-right arc must remain unresolved; do not silently give the complete circle a certified solid/dashed style.
- The exact printed presence/absence of obscured negative and y = +/-2 labels is not certified.
- Cap-junction heights are known only by their grid interval from this view. Exact analytic intersection coordinates require an approved figure interpretation, not an answer key.

## Fig. 27b: right panel

### Grid, axes, and labels

- One rectangular coordinate panel, approximately x pixels 916 to 1721 and y pixels 39 to 595; origin approximately (1321, 313).
- Grid extent: 18 columns, 12 rows; origin column 9 / row 6, half-unit subdivisions, zero phase. Left-side hatching masks portions of the lattice, while the right half exposes the row/column count.
