- Readable labels: y at the top, x at the right edge, origin 0/O below/right of the axis intersection, x = 1 beneath the descending line's x-axis crossing.
- A y = 1 glyph appears just left of the two-line junction above the origin but is crowded by hatch, the y axis, and diagonal strokes. Its reading is less secure than the separate x = 1 label.
- No other positive or negative numeric labels are confidently readable.
- Source `б)` is in the lower-left corner and overlaps nearby hatched/diagonal structure.

### Boundaries, region, and endpoints

- Two complete diagonal straight lines cross on the vertical axis, two half-unit rows above the horizontal axis.
- The descending line runs from a top-edge position two coordinate units left of the y axis, through the junction, through the x-axis crossing at x = 1, to a bottom-edge position four coordinate units right of the y axis.
- The ascending line runs from a bottom-edge position four coordinate units left of the y axis, through the x-axis crossing one coordinate unit left of the origin, through the same junction, to a top-edge position two coordinate units right of the y axis.
- The corresponding unlabeled grid relationships are: junction (0, 1); descending clips near (-2, 3) and (4, -3); ascending clips near (-4, -3) and (2, 3). Clip positions are approximate because of frame thickness.
- Both lines continue on both sides of their crossing; the right-side continuations are unhatched but visibly present. They must not be reduced to two leftward rays.
- Both visible diagonal spines look solid; no regular intended dashed pattern is discernible. Print/grid junctions create local gaps and changes in apparent thickness.
- The hatched set is the left-facing wedge between the two lines, ending at their common junction. It lies above the ascending line and below the descending line on the left side.
- The upper-right and lower-right sectors, the right-facing wedge, and the regions left of the junction but outside the two lines are not hatched.
- Hatching rises steeply to the right. It is clipped by the wedge and panel edges, not a third mathematical boundary.
- No isolated point marker at the junction, endpoint circles/dots, or boundary-line arrowheads are discernible. Any sharp tip at the right x-axis crossing is a stroke junction, not a certified arrowhead.

